Z||000011||Jncomutt||08-24-2001||01:29 PM||||Ok, heres where I stand.  I am currently holding the entire light assembly that goes in the window.  I also have the approximately 8 foot wire that ran from the plug on the light, down the driver side pillar, under the seat, across the support beam thing (wasn't a box), to the ebrake, where another connector plug comes off, and up to behind the fuse box where I unplugged it.  When I looked at the plug on my 84, its different/not there.  SO, again, i'm at a loss, what do I do now????||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fierokid87||08-24-2001||01:33 PM||||Ok well you could do this.  do you have a Haynes manual?  If so look up the tail light wiring and follow that for the break lights.  Then splice in to the break lights wiring.  Im going to be at my Fiero today Ill check it out for you.  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phil||08-24-2001||02:28 PM||||The easiest and most sure fire way of wiring the 3rd brake light is to run a wire directly from the brake switch( by the pedal) to the 3rd light. That way you don't have to mess with the blinker system.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Max The Chainsaw||08-24-2001||02:35 PM||||Phil's got the easiest way. You need to hook one wire from the light to a ground somewhere. Then route the other to the brake light switch. Find the wire on the brake light switch that is hot only when the pedal is pressed and hook to it.<P>This will bypass any thing the turn signal switch does to the rear lights and makes sure the light only comes on when you press the brakes.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Jncomutt||08-24-2001||02:43 PM||||Thanks alot guys, I'm going to go hook this up now... I'll keep ya updated...||||reg||1
